Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?P27:=20KIPLATFORM::IO::SeqFOpen=20=E2=80=94=20t?=
 =?UTF-8?q?reat=20posix=5Ffadvise=20failure=20as=20harmless?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The old code closed the file and returned nullptr if posix_fadvise
failed. posix_fadvise is advisory — its failure never invalidates the
FILE*. Consequence: on filesystems where fadvise isn't supported
(some FUSE mounts, network filesystems, in-VM overlays) SeqFOpen
returned NULL for a perfectly openable file, and every FILE_LINE_READER
consumer threw 'Unable to open %s for reading' — an error message with
no relation to the actual cause. Board load, footprint load, drawing
sheet load, DRC rules load, netlist load all break for the same
reason on affected filesystems.

Ignore fadvise failure. If the OS can't optimise read-ahead, it just
uses the default heuristic — the FILE* is still fine.
---
 libs/kiplatform/os/unix/io.cpp | 16 +++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libs/kiplatform/os/unix/io.cpp b/libs/kiplatform/os/unix/io.cpp
index 6018bb92..58b1bff3 100644
--- a/libs/kiplatform/os/unix/io.cpp
+++ b/libs/kiplatform/os/unix/io.cpp
@@ -42,11 +42,17 @@ FILE* KIPLATFORM::IO::SeqFOpen( const wxString& aPath, const wxString& aMode )
 
     if( fp )
     {
-        if( posix_fadvise( fileno( fp ), 0, 0, POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL ) != 0 )
-        {
-            fclose( fp );
-            fp = nullptr;
-        }
+        // posix_fadvise is a hint for the kernel's read-ahead heuristic. On
+        // some filesystems (older kernels + FUSE, some in-VM overlays, and
+        // certain network mounts) it returns EINVAL / EBADF / EOPNOTSUPP
+        // even though the FILE* is perfectly usable. The old code closed
+        // the file on that error, then FILE_LINE_READER's ctor threw
+        // "Unable to open %s for reading" — a totally misleading error
+        // that made board load fail on those filesystems.
+        //
+        // Fadvise is advisory; on failure the OS just uses its default
+        // read-ahead policy. Never destroy the fp because of it.
+        (void) posix_fadvise( fileno( fp ), 0, 0, POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL );
     }
 
     return fp;
